<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/031d7033b75af3cd9d23b99c39416158db42e9b14afab669eedca87e3c443cca> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Offerings at a tomb; in center, a garlanded stele with pediment on two steps; at r. a woman in a chiton with a cloak thrown over her l. shoulder, on the l. a draped youth leaning on a staff)" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/0bcf388c738fdae04b967fc66ed14c9b0c63a55462e4528446457ac10e7734da> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Fogg Museum Classical Collection" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/0e2218b1f33af3b6438482f2281b1a4a7cdb1f0fbf190a9fc9ec13ab084bf2ae> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Frederick M. Watkins; Bequest to the Fogg Art Museum, Harvard University, 1972.

In addition, a handwritten note in the 1973 catalogue written by David Mitten quotes a March 5, 1973 letter from Dietrich von Bothmer: "On loan to MFA in 1912. Sold by the widow of Richard Norton to Morley."
[Andreya Mihaloew 4/28/2008]""" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/10ae432a9ff74c4abdae9baf34858e34a51d37a1a62f2a5fce261bbe409707ad> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Obv.: Herakles stringing bow
Rev.: Studded wheel; around: foru dolphins. The whole in incuse square""" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1727fe3a25065cab3aff46322e81500710f292b549d6dbbc4520fce997329759> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Commentary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Satyrs and maenads are frequently depicted with each other since they are both regular companions of the god Dionysos. Their interactions generally center around the satyr’s sexual pursuit of the maenad. The satyr is intended to be a comic figure in that despite his overly exaggerated sexual arousal, he is never able to satisfy his desire since he is never able to successfully capture a woman." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1b2fb46261bee89e3ff56eb8801e1b15c38275474952f1c764e33bade14870d9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Credit line"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Harvard Art Museums/Arthur M. Sackler Museum, Gift of Dr. and Mrs. Jerome M. Eisenberg" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2040abb6db306db1e85cb7cd46a76b22904411f81b76759dbc509b03e53611e6> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Credit line"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Harvard Art Museums/Arthur M. Sackler Museum, Bequest of Joseph C. Hoppin" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2c7744f8484d5c627e4c857151a86d3efa6daefdef561e1ef2c9e5b5072f2312> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Credit line"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Harvard Art Museums/Arthur M. Sackler Museum, Gift of Münzen und Medaillen A.G." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3048dbddb548660e70cce1e974706c508876f3ecc7ff4966cd80d861e880846f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Jacob Hirsch (said to come from Gela). Frederick M. Watkins, 1941, bequest; to Fogg Art Museum, 1972." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/31eea9be8118c50baae9bcdd04acfa989cf12d4d5e485348bd8457c345263305> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Molded in relief with a feline head emerging from a scroll." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/351a40968408a12c30b44005b55573fe0e40a5f41548205fecf80a2444fd5c3d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Found at Vulci. Campanari Collection (1833). Durand Collection (1833-1836) sold; to Claude Camille Rollin, Paris, (1836). Purchase (1993) Jerome Eisenberg, Royal Athena Galleries, New York, [Nanterre Sale], sold; to Harvard University Art Museums, 1994." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/35bff1ad419652ac0bf1c9cd105a26bb58d213f74040493e66573a381def4fd3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Woman dressed in a long, pleated chiton.  Himation comes over her head beneath a plain calathus.  Offering in her left hand.  Oval face, framed by curly hair.  Long nose bent inward at the tip and a pouting mouth.  Solid clay with an unmodeled back.  Chip on left side.
Fairly soft, play reddish brown clay; traces of white slip and red pigment.""" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a4c765ea8902c7d32406447de278b4283529bf57566951aeb28a54cfa6994cb> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Credit line"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Harvard Art Museums/Arthur M. Sackler Museum, Gift of E. P. Warren, Esquire" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3bce42c41f21a11a58e322d4f5124c303ff927305f0c0118017c7866a7cfc874> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Edward Perry Warren, Esq., London, (by 1927-1928), gift; to Fogg Art Museum, 1927." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/40fb0472c886cfb8173283d5e7102fc4a206b920af9d8e92f7c7c597ac23155a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Bought at Athens by George Pfeiffer.
Gift of George Pfeiffer to the Harvard Department of the Classics, October 1905.
Transfer from the Department of the Classics, 1977.""" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/440cb48db601cef23066b3fb520d982a4cadb30375e3d8739d4cb2d46039f89d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Helmeted warrior to right with shield emblem of bearded satyr head in high relief." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/46b4d0d44e6f2308152bdb87db0d32274fcbd7ae99e6de515eb9c10792f17c34> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Fragment composed of three joining pieces from the bowl of a kylix.  On the exterior, to the left is a trace of the kylix handle root with a relief line marking off the reserve area between the handles. Interior:  meander to the left; exterior (Side A):  under handle root of left handle, leg of a stool; lower part of a male figure wearing a himation, leaning of a staff. Relief contours, preliminary sketch, dilute glaze musculature. Shoe on right foot of figure facing right." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4f77c2903f79705641b9d70894457e5a28e39ab6493532e021e7f801874c955b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Seiden & de Cuevas, Inc., New York, New York. on Long Term Loan at the Fogg Art Museum from 1977 to 1982" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5b6e2d123722a3ad124d513dd565efdf42617a8962097bca93276a0e81ef0f3c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Richard R. Wagner, gift; to the Fogg Museum, 1968." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/644f887adb51c69357ed0e29aa8b0d95e1431d725442af70ee67685bc355eba3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Credit line"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Harvard Art Museums/Arthur M. Sackler Museum, Gift of Professor C.E. Norton and Richard Norton" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/67a227d2fdf015a763e4c49167b8be1013090c84587ff41724882e6fd691281d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Exterior: figure to right (Herakles?), feline paw to right; interior: decoration of contour lines on reserve clay. Thickness at middle 0.4 cm." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/782697664e9e945fa9d55151d612fa93f1e61c619446338936e493306b6dc7ec> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Credit line"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Harvard Art Museums/Arthur M. Sackler Museum, Transfer from the Department of the Classics, Harvard University, Gift of Prof J M. Paton And Dr. A.S. Pease." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7929cfcfea79bdf81d3cf491561f645b1aedc172bc37ee048ab5c1a92e2c6c9e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Interior: In exergue, pair of cocks facing; above snake; horse, bird at top of tondo.
Side A: rays at join of foot and bowl, black glaze bands, palmettes.""" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7a9868f99ee385d99690158a03864a996fe21b96ed33c8f5bbf82c2ba5e4cbad> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Credit line"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Harvard Art Museums/Arthur M. Sackler Museum, Loan from Estate of Donald Upham and Mrs. R. U. Hunter" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7dfb312b30539b23465506578737d75f9510505ac5c83f84193cd44d475331b9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Broken at shoulder, with upper part missing. Black graze is abraded. Incisions and drawing are hasty and careless. At rim, a zone of dots between two narrow bands. On body, a figure wearing chiton and himation and fillet in hair (krobylos), preparing to mount a chariot drawn by four horses, holding a goad in his right hand and reins in his left; one foot is in the chariot. Behind the horses, a winged figure in long drapery (indistinct); at horses' heads a siren wearing a short chiton, holding a horse's head in left hand. Below design, narrow and broad black bands. The lower two-thirds of the vase, except part of rim of foot, is solid black. White on horses'  manes and tails, and wings and tails, on wings and chiton of winged figure." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/89b87c83e6fa40de5b54f0fbbe83c2864af74442eed7cc7c4fc5bc7a08afd850> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A-F: six joining center fragments (catalogue \"A\"); G-H: two joining rim fragments (catalogue \"B\"); I and J: two separate rim fragments (catalogue \"C\" and \"D\")." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9552bc8ea21f2f497538c547d76ded7015e2c904a3ca45c475e21d06b5f25b1a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Pinched faced rider clasping the crested mane of his lively steed, left arm missing. Traces of matt black painted stripes on horse. Legs and tail missing. Tip of muzzle missing; neck broken off and re-attached." .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9cf790996497ab31d4ac3c8e457b1c78b813abe58daa84d7b6eb7847c51ca784> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Commentary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """This little jug or pitcher depicts a boy feeding, teasing, or training a dog with food. This miniature pitcher shape is called a “chous” in ancient Greek. Made in Athens, this vessel type was popular in the late 5th century BCE.
These little pitchers are connected to a spring festival in Athens called the Anthesteria, a festival of Dionysos that was especially connected to the new wine of the year and the arrival of spring. Some scholars have suggested that these little pitchers were used in festival ritual for the first taste of wine for three-year old children. They are often decorated, appropriately, with child-related themes such as this.
The little dog is a type known in antiquity as a Melitan (Maltese) dog. It is commonly depicted as a pet in ancient Greek art (see the grave stele of Melisto, 1961.86, for another depiction of a child teasing a Melitan dog in the collection).""" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/abecdd8b3ce76382686392b7b65027f2674bfdb694f678e5c5965a06465096b9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """On the interior of this cup, a maenad (female follower of Dionysos) and a satyr dance facing each other, their arms outstretched and thrown up into the air. On the left, the maenad bows her head. She is wreathed with ivy and wears a long chiton (tunic) with many creases and folds. Her bent legs are visibly outlined through the fabric, suggesting its fineness and translucence. It is tied by a thin belt with tassels painted on in red. A leopard skin, an animal sacred to Dionysos, is draped over her upraised arm. In front of her, a nude satyr dances in a similar fashion, extended his right arm (partially preserved) in front of him and his left over his head. He also wears a red wreath of ivy leaves. His beard is long and hairy. The tip of his erection points upward. 

The exterior of this cup is decorated with a flurry of pursuit scenes where highly aroused satyrs attempt to court the fleeing and resistant maenads. The satyrs wear ivy wreaths, as do some of the maenads. Some of the maenads instead wear slim fillets tied around their hair. All the satyrs have leopard skins draped over their arms as they reach out in attempt to grab the maenads. All the maenads wear similar translucent chitons tied by a red belt, and two of them wear leopard skins, this time fastened at the neck. Their bodies are outlined beneath their clothing, revealing their various twists and poses as they resist the satyrs’ advances. Each carries a thyrsos, an ivy topped staff associated with Dionysos. On one side, a satyr even tries to grab the thyrsos to take it away from the maenad. On the other side, a maenad extends it in front of her, appearing to use it as a weapon against her pursuer. Behind her, a satyr caries a drinking horn, often a symbol of excessive wine consumption, which is emphasized by the wineskin hanging behind him in the background. A long-horned goat walks beneath the handle on one side, mirrored by a crawling satyr on the other, offering a humorous comparison between the satyr and the animal.""" .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b61f721900e098c456833bac3b3160c8a8d4900e379886a816088f439dfaee48> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Credit line"@en ;
